r="ltr"><li>Phylum: Apicomplexa</li><li>Class: Sporozoa</li><li>Order: Haemosporida</li><li>Genus: Plasmodium</li><li>4 species: vivax, ovale, malariae, falciparum</li></ul></details><details class="es"><summary><span class="st">★</span> Why is P. falciparum named so?</summary><ul dir="ltr"><li>Crescentic gametocytes</li><li>Crescent, sausage, banana shaped</li></ul></details><details class="es"><summary><span class="st">★</span> Distribution of P. malariae and P. falciparum.</summary><ul dir="ltr"><li>P. malariae: tropical Africa, Sri Lanka, Burma, parts of India</li><li>P. falciparum: tropical, subtropical regions</li></ul></details></section>
<section class="rv" id="quiz"><h2>🎯 اختبار MCQ</h2><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q1.</b> P. falciparum is named after:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">Crescent gametocytes</button><button class="op" data-j="1">Band form</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Rosette schizont</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Schüffner's dots</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Crescentic shape of gametocytes.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q2.</b> Band form is a unique feature of:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">P. malariae</button><button class="op" data-j="1">P. falciparum</button><button class="op" data-j="2">P. vivax</button><button class="op" data-j="3">P. ovale</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Cytoplasm elongates across the RBC.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q3.</b> P. malariae causes:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">Benign quartan malaria</button><button class="op" data-j="1">Malignant tertian malaria</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Blackwater fever</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Cerebral malaria</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Fever every 4th day (quartan).</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q4.</b> Schizont of P. malariae has usually:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">8-10 merozoites</button><button class="op" data-j="1">16 merozoites</button><button class="op" data-j="2">24 merozoites</button><button class="op" data-j="3">2 merozoites</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">6-12, usually 8-10, in a rosette.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q5.</b> Schizont of P. falciparum has usually:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">16 merozoites</button><button class="op" data-j="1">8-10 merozoites</button><button class="op" data-j="2">6 merozoites</button><button class="op" data-j="3">30,000 merozoites</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">8-24, usually 16.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q6.</b> Schizonts are rarely seen in blood of:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">P. falciparum</button><button class="op" data-j="1">P. malariae</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Both</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Neither</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Except severe cases.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q7.</b> Binucleate ring resembling stereo headphones:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">P. falciparum</button><button class="op" data-j="1">P. malariae</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Both</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Neither</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Double chromatin, common.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q8.</b> Falciparum gametocytes appear after ring stage by about:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">10 days</button><button class="op" data-j="1">2 days</button><button class="op" data-j="2">30 days</button><button class="op" data-j="3">1 hour</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">~10 days.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q9.</b> Definitive host of Plasmodium:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">Female Anopheles</button><button class="op" data-j="1">Man</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Cow</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Dog</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Sexual phase occurs in the mosquito.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q10.</b> Best film to differentiate species:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">Thin film</button><button class="op" data-j="1">Thick film</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Both</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Neither</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Thick film recognizes parasite presence.</div></div><div class="q" data-a="0"><p dir="ltr"><b>Q11.</b> Order of Plasmodium:</p><div class="ops" dir="ltr"><button class="op c" data-j="0">Haemosporida</button><button class="op" data-j="1">Apicomplexa</button><button class="op" data-j="2">Sporozoa</button><button class="op" data-j="3">Coccidia</button></div><div class="why" dir="ltr">Phylum Apicomplexa, class Sporozoa, order Haemosporida.</div></div></section>
<section class="rv" id="gold"><h2>⭐ الملخص الذهبي <small dir="ltr">Golden sheet</small></h2><div class="gg"><div class="gc g1"><h5>🧬 1. Classification</h5><div dir="ltr">Phylum: Apicomplexa · Class: Sporozoa · Order: Haemosporida · Genus: Plasmodium · 4 human species: vivax, ovale, malariae, falciparum</div></div><div class="gc g2"><h5>🌍 2. P. malariae: general</h5><div dir="ltr">Distribution: tropical Africa, Sri Lanka, Burma, parts of India · Disease: benign quartan malaria · Definitive host: female Anopheles (vector) · Intermediate host: man · Transmission: bite of female Anopheles</div></div><div class="gc g3"><h5>⭕ 3. P. malariae: ring &amp; band form</h5><div dir="ltr">Ring: resembles P. vivax, thicker, intensely stained · Single large chromatin dot · No enlargement of infected RBC · Band form: unique feature of P. malariae · Numerous large pigment granules</div></div><div class="gc g4"><h5>💥 4. P. malariae: schizont &amp; gametocyte</h5><div dir="ltr">Schizont: 6-12 merozoites (usually 8-10) · Arranged: rosette or irregular cluster · Schizonts can be common in peripheral blood · Mature schizont nearly fills normal-sized RBC · Gametocytes: compact, fill host RBC</div></div><div class="gc g0"><h5>🌙 5. P. falciparum: general</h5><div dir="ltr">Name: crescentic shape of gametocytes · Distribution: tropical, subtropical regions · Disease: malignant tertian malaria, often fatal · Definitive host: female Anopheles (vector) · Intermediate host: man</div></div><div class="gc g1"><h5>🎧 6. P. falciparum: ring form</h5><div dir="ltr">Early ring: very delicate, tiny · Rings attached along margin of RBC · Binucleate rings (double chromatin): stereo headphones · Several rings in single erythrocyte · Usually no enlargement of infected RBC</div></div><div class="gc g2"><h5>🌙 7. P. falciparum: schizont &amp; gametocyte</h5><div dir="ltr">Mature schizont: smallest of all species · Rarely seen in peripheral blood, except severe cases · Merozoites: 8-24, usually 16 · Fills about 2/3 of infected RBC · Gametocytes: crescentic, sausage, banana shaped</div></div><div class="gc g3"><h5>🔄 8. Life cycle</h5><div dir="ltr">2 phases · Asexual phase: humans (intermediate host) · Sexual phase: mosquito (definitive host)</div></div><div class="gc g4"><h5>🔬 9. Laboratory diagnosis</h5><div dir="ltr">Thick film: recognize presence of parasite · Light infection, endemic area: easy, fast · Thin film: differentiate between species · Serodiagnosis</div></div><div class="gc g0"><h5>⚖️ 10. Comparison</h5><div dir="ltr">P. malariae: benign quartan, band form, schizont 6-12 merozoites · P. falciparum: malignant tertian, crescent gametocytes, schizont 8-24 merozoites · Schizonts: common in blood (malariae) vs rare (falciparum) · Both: usually no RBC enlargement</div></div></div><div class="gt"><div class="tw"><h4 dir="ltr">P. malariae vs P. falciparum</h4><table dir="ltr"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>P. malariae</th><th>P. falciparum</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disease</td><td>Benign quartan</td><td>Malignant tertian, often fatal</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Tropical Africa, Sri Lanka, Burma, parts of India</td><td>Tropical, subtropical</td></tr><tr><td>Ring</td><td>Thick, intensely stained, single large dot</td><td>Delicate, tiny, margin; double chromatin; several per RBC</td></tr><tr><td>Unique</td><td>Band form, large pigment</td><td>Crescent gametocyte</td></tr><tr><td>Schizont merozoites</td><td>6-12 (usually 8-10), rosette</td><td>8-24 (usually 16), fills 2/3 RBC</td></tr><tr><td>Schizont in blood</td><td>Can be common</td><td>Rare, except severe cases</td></tr><tr><td>Gametocyte</td><td>Compact, fills RBC</td><td>Crescent, sausage, banana; 1.5x RBC</td></tr><tr><td>RBC enlargement</td><td>None</td><td>Usually none</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="tw"><h4 dir="ltr">Classification</h4><table dir="ltr"><thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Phylum</td><td>Apicomplexa</td></tr><tr><td>Class</td><td>Sporozoa</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td>Haemosporida</td></tr><tr><td>Genus</td><td>Plasmodium (4 human species)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="tw"><h4 dir="ltr">Life cycle &amp; diagnosis</h4><table dir="ltr"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asexual phase</td><td>Humans (intermediate host)</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual phase</td><td>Mosquito (definitive host)</td></tr><tr><td>Thick film</td><td>Detect parasite; light infection, endemic area</td></tr><tr><td>Thin film</td><td>Differentiate species</td></tr><tr><td>Serodiagnosis</td><td>Listed in lecture</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>
